{"kind":"request","text":"Coherence review of dap: five items for the planner to disposition, one deferred\n\nSource. Kristen Maevyn, \"The Decoherence Trap\", https://maevyn.codes/decoherence, 14 October 2026. Its claims: incoherence grows with change, not with time; a half-finished transition is the worst kind of debt, because only someone who knows both the old and the new form can judge whether a change is safe; a system that is measured only by itself cannot tell improvement from drift. Hugh read it on 22 October 2026, asked for a review of dap through it, and then asked for this request. He accepted items 1, 2, 3, 5 and 6 for the planner to disposition as it sees fit, and deferred item 4.\n\nWhat the review observed, on main dd6e69ae on 22 October 2026, by scripts over the Git history and the tree:\n\n- 3,917 commits since 1 October. Under packages/, 400,080 lines added and 296,787 removed, against 176,216 lines of TypeScript now under packages/ and apps/.\n- 66 design notes under notes/ since 1 October, and 16 distinct status forms across notes/ and notes/plans/.\n- Package names: 7 packages are named @generalbusiness/artroom-* and 6 are named @generalbusiness/dap-*. Under packages/, 733 files contain the word artroom and 771 contain dap.\n- Old and new forms live side by side in the hot path: application install v1 and v2 (packages/platform/src/application-install.ts and application-install-v2.ts); MCP wire revisions modern and legacy (packages/mcp/src/wire.ts:125); receiver reads in legacy and partition modes (packages/scope/src/receiver-reads.ts); archive targets in legacy, room and all modes (packages/scope/src/archive-target.ts); standing destination versions 5 and 6 (packages/platform/src/versions.ts); 19 pinned platform versions across six definitions in packages/platform/src/native-catalog.ts, and replay needs the code for every one (docs/kernel.md, K1).\n- docs/kernel.md lists eleven parts that fail the kernel test, K1 to K11. The sprint report of 22 October commits the planner to file K9, and K2, K3 and K11 as one item.\n- 286 commit subjects since 1 October are longer than 300 characters, and 22 contain words glued to hashes. Example, commit 13193f31: \"exactb8a4dea1/tree73587f1b, all126reviewedSourcepaths under independentapproval9b751d7b\".\n- The sprint report of 22 October records the anchor bound decided twice with conflicting rulings (066e49b3 and f15e483c, replaced by a7d03336).\n\nThe three OS-lens notes that landed on 22 October (the kernel page, the one-channel audit and definition succession) are the kind of work the essay prescribes: a small part held stable, every side channel named, and a way to change a definition without reinterpreting history. The items below are about the rest.\n\nItems.\n\n1. No new paradigm while the kernel list grows. Make docs/kernel.md a filing gate: a design note or request that adds a scope kind, a platform name, a capability, a tool family or a new model of the record waits while K1 to K11 are open, or states which K item it closes. The planner decides the exact rule, how it applies to the K filings it has already committed to, and how it applies to work already in flight (Counter slices 2 to 6, history slice H1, the layout note, the Room fork, OAuth, the guard grammar).\n\n2. Finish the artroom to dap rename in package names. Seven packages still carry the old name. Decide whether the published names change (docs/public-sdk-release.md names the public set) and file one builder request for the rename. Internal identifiers may keep artroom where a change would alter recorded bytes, as the rename plan 028 allowed.\n\n3. Freeze code that no live scope pins. For application install v1, the legacy MCP session revision, legacy receiver reads, and standing destination versions 5 and 6: check which deployed scopes (the artroom-scope, dap-grow and dap-lending cohorts named in the sprint report of 22 October) pin or use each; then move the code for versions that no live scope pins to the archived path that K1's first option describes, or delete it where nothing recorded needs it. This item rests on the K1 disposition and must keep kernel promise 2: every entry form ever written stays replayable.\n\n4. Deferred. One outside reader of a dap room, as a cut-down form of the plan 032 exit test. Hugh: wait until the journey is delivered and the grow demo is validated. Record the precondition; no work now.\n\n5. Bound commit messages in the gate. A cap on subject length and a check for words glued to hashes, in scripts/gate.sh or in a check the checker runs on every head, with the planner choosing the bound. Kernel promise 1 says the record is the interface, and the Git log is part of the record.\n\n6. One status form for notes and plans. Reduce the 16 status forms found across notes/ and notes/plans/ to the four forms of the design-note status convention. The sweep at 25323213 covered the plans; the notes still vary. A document-only head, checker review before landing.","body":{"conditions":"For each of items 1, 2, 3, 5 and 6, the planner records one disposition as an assert resting on this request: the ID of the builder request it filed, the existing request it folds into, or the reason it declines the item. Item 4 is recorded as deferred with its precondition (the journey delivered and the grow demo validated by a person). Every head that results follows the usual route: checker review at the exact head before landing. Plain English per ISO 24495-1 for a technical audience; no product names other than GitHub. No item changes recorded bytes or the meaning of any recorded entry.","priority":"planner's judgment; items 2, 5 and 6 are cheap; item 1 is a filing rule that takes effect on disposition; item 3 rests on the K1 disposition","target":"inherit","to":"20db43a245e9780b92a587425d387d393b17ffc90b89e036ff752399f88fc517"}}